chord — third-party material, and what it is not
================================================

chord is MIT licensed (see LICENSE).  Two pieces of it come from elsewhere, and
one thing that a text-to-speech engine usually contains is deliberately absent.


data/cmudict.dict — the CMU Pronouncing Dictionary
--------------------------------------------------

Copyright (C) 1993-2015 Carnegie Mellon University.  Redistributed under the
2-clause BSD licence reproduced in full at data/LICENSE.cmudict, which permits
use and redistribution — including commercially — with attribution.

    http://www.speech.cs.cmu.edu/cgi-bin/cmudict
    https://github.com/cmusphinx/cmudict

It is the authority for how chord says any word it lists, and the training data
from which src/lts-data.lisp — the rules for words it does not list — is learned
by tools/learn-lts.lisp.  Nothing else was used to build those rules.


Voices
------

chord runs Piper VITS voices (https://github.com/rhasspy/piper), which are
distributed separately under their own licences; no voice is included here.  A
voice's phoneme_id_map defines the IPA inventory src/arpabet.lisp targets.


No espeak, and why the absence is deliberate
--------------------------------------------

The usual way to turn English text into phonemes is espeak-ng, directly or
through piper-phonemize.  espeak-ng is GPLv3, and a transcription of its rules
or a redistribution of its compiled dictionary makes the resulting work GPLv3
too.  chord therefore does not use, link, transcribe, or ship any part of it —
the front end is CMUdict plus rules learned from CMUdict, which is why chord can
be MIT.

Note for anyone comparing output: because Piper voices were trained on
espeak-ng's transcription, chord's ARPAbet-to-IPA table (src/arpabet.lisp) aims
at that same IPA inventory.  Aiming at a target is not deriving from it — the
table is a statement about US English transcription convention, written from
published phonetic description and checked against the voice's own symbol list.
